WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.600
Christopher Riley, Screenwriter, Author,&nbsp;
and Instructor: And as we turned into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:09.840
entrance to the ER we looked at each other and I&nbsp;
think Kathy was the one who said “In this moment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:12.560
our lives are changing forever.”

00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:17.360
Film Courage: You and Kathy have&nbsp;
a new book that just came out.

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:20.880
Christopher: The Defining How&nbsp;
Writers and Actors Build Characters.

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:25.280
Film Courage: Great and we're&nbsp;
wondering if there are certain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:29.600
chapters or a portion of the book&nbsp;
that you can reference? First off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:34.320
a moment or moments that damage or cripple&nbsp;
a character. Do you have any references?

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:44.560
Christopher: We do because that's something that&nbsp;
is critically important in storytelling because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.560 --> 00:00:52.320
we're so often dealing with characters who are&nbsp;
damaged or crippled so Kathy and I talk about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.480 --> 00:01:02.480
from the film Finding Nemo the way Nemo's dad&nbsp;
Marlin is damaged by the tragic loss of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:12.640
family to a barracuda but we also try to share&nbsp;
some of our own defining moments because we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:21.920
urge storytellers writers actors directors&nbsp;
to delve into their own histories to find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:27.840
the moments that have defined them in this case&nbsp;
moments that have damaged wounded crippled them.

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:36.240
In the history of our family when we think&nbsp;
of defining moments there's one moment that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:39.840
stands above all the others&nbsp;
and it was the moment when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:48.480
our son Peter as a five and a half year old&nbsp;
little boy was diagnosed with a brain tumor and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:59.280
our pediatrician put the MRI films in our hands&nbsp;
and said you need to drive to UCLA medical center&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:05.760
a brain surgeon will be waiting for&nbsp;
you and he'll explain everything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:12.800
and so Kathy and I got in the car and oh and the&nbsp;
doctor said “And don't stop anywhere on the way.”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:22.480
We put our son in the car, drove to the ER and as&nbsp;
we turned into the entrance to the ER we looked at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:30.560
each other and I think Kathy was the one who said&nbsp;
“In this moment, our lives are changing forever”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:39.520
and that was true. We now in our family&nbsp;
talk about the moment before Peter got sick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:49.520
and we talk about the moment after Peter's brain&nbsp;
tumor. My daughter's life experience was different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:59.840
from that moment on her best friend and brother&nbsp;
suddenly was out of her life and in the hospital&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:07.360
and she wasn't seeing him and when she did&nbsp;
finally see him after surgery he was changed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:20.640
the tumor was gone but he didn't speak didn't&nbsp;
walk he looked to her like a monster and

00:03:23.440 --> 00:03:29.920
for Peter that was a defining moment it was a&nbsp;
moment that crippled him that made him and Peter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.800 --> 00:03:38.400
many years later is alive but as a result&nbsp;
of the surgery radiation which isn't good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:46.560
for little boys brains and chemotherapy he's&nbsp;
kind of a real-life forest gump pure of heart&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:59.840
and and he knows what love is&nbsp;
and he struggles intellectually

00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:09.920
so that was a moment of wounding for him it&nbsp;
was the beginning of the death of the dreams&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:20.720
that we had for him we had to find new dreams to&nbsp;
replace the ones that died and it took a long time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:27.840
though we were the ones who were dragged&nbsp;
kicking and screaming to recognizing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:31.360
the dreams that were not&nbsp;
going to happen now for him

00:04:33.760 --> 00:04:36.560
even as we approached his 16th birthday and

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:41.840
people his age were going to get driver's licenses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:48.480
none of us wants to live in a world where Peter&nbsp;
Riley has a driver's license because he just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.480 --> 00:04:55.680
cannot process all of the information fast enough&nbsp;
and he would get it right 95 percent of the time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.680 --> 00:05:04.480
but that's not that's not enough on the streets&nbsp;
of la and so I had to eventually say to him Peter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.160 --> 00:05:13.360
you're not going to get a driver's license and so&nbsp;
he's found a new dream he heard about the Google&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.360 --> 00:05:18.960
car and so he's all about a self-driving car&nbsp;
as soon as he can get one he wants a car that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.960 --> 00:05:33.040
going to take him wherever it can take him we had&nbsp;
to absorb a lot of of losses but a day came when

00:05:35.680 --> 00:05:39.360
Peter had struggled along&nbsp;
in school got to high school&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.720 --> 00:05:49.440
and in California there's a test you have to pass&nbsp;
in order to get a diploma called the California&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:56.160
high school exit exam it's a standardized test so&nbsp;
no matter how much people at the school loved him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.720 --> 00:06:01.360
and helped him there was no way he was going&nbsp;
to get a diploma if he didn't pass this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.080 --> 00:06:10.160
test and I remember the day that the results came&nbsp;
in the mail and I just heard Kathy screaming in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.160 --> 00:06:13.600
the other part of the house she'd open&nbsp;
the envelope and he had passed by like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.640 --> 00:06:20.800
a whisker and and so we got to&nbsp;
go to his high school graduation

00:06:23.120 --> 00:06:29.200
and I’ve been in high school and I know how it&nbsp;
works people applaud based on how popular you are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.240 --> 00:06:36.720
when your name is called and so my family had&nbsp;
all come to his graduation and I was telling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.720 --> 00:06:42.400
everybody you've got to like cheer your brains&nbsp;
out because we have to be as loud as possible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.080 --> 00:06:47.840
and Kathy was just saying oh I just I don't&nbsp;
care about that I just hope he doesn't fall&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.840 --> 00:06:55.680
off the stage and they called his name and&nbsp;
the entire senior class came to their feet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.680 --> 00:07:03.280
applauding because they all recognized what&nbsp;
he had overcome in order to earn that diploma

00:07:05.360 --> 00:07:10.320
Kathy and I look at that experience as a&nbsp;
defining moment for us that was a moment of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.320 --> 00:07:18.720
healing it was a moment where we got back some&nbsp;
of what we had lost and Peter still struggles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.640 --> 00:07:24.720
he had another defining moment where&nbsp;
just in an afternoon he lost his hearing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.440 --> 00:07:35.440
and that happened recently further isolated&nbsp;
him and made his life harder made our lives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.440 --> 00:07:42.880
harder it's really hard to raise your voice so&nbsp;
that he can hear us and not feel angry when you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.880 --> 00:07:49.440
shouting it's your body won't disconnect shouting&nbsp;
and anger and so we're always struggling with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.440 --> 00:07:57.840
like how do I talk loudly enough for him to hear&nbsp;
me and not have to apologize for it afterwards

00:08:02.400 --> 00:08:12.320
we are not unique in experiencing moments of&nbsp;
loss one of the things that experiencing loss has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.320 --> 00:08:20.960
sensitized us to is that everyone experiences&nbsp;
losses and often those losses happen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.960 --> 00:08:25.840
out of view they're not visible&nbsp;
when we go to the grocery store&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.360 --> 00:08:31.520
and we believe it's true of our characters as well

00:08:33.760 --> 00:08:44.480
I think that you can't work with characters in&nbsp;
this way without becoming more compassionate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.400 --> 00:08:56.480
without becoming aware of what people are&nbsp;
suffering and so in that way I hope this book is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.520 --> 00:09:05.200
is a humanizing force within our industry and&nbsp;
helps people gain more compassion for one another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.840 --> 00:09:14.880
and also for their characters and maybe through&nbsp;
their characters even the audience will will grow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.880 --> 00:09:25.760
in the kind of compassion and sensitivity and&nbsp;
humanity that movies helped to bring to me our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.760 --> 00:09:30.480
business could probably do with some of that&nbsp;
our world could probably do with some of that

00:09:33.280 --> 00:09:38.160
Do you have a favorite chapter&nbsp;
you and Kathy definitely I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.800 --> 00:09:46.240
you know I wrote a book about&nbsp;
script format a few years ago and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.440 --> 00:09:53.040
this is a very different kind of&nbsp;
book because so much of our heart&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.040 --> 00:10:01.120
is on the page I think probably the the&nbsp;
chapter that guides the reader through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.120 --> 00:10:06.960
discovering their own defining moments is my&nbsp;
favorite chapter it's the chapter where we share&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.960 --> 00:10:14.960
many of our own moments sometimes big&nbsp;
moments like the brain tumor moment but also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.960 --> 00:10:26.800
smaller quieter moments that have also shaped us&nbsp;
we do that as an encouragement to our readers to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.800 --> 00:10:32.480
be willing to look and find their own defining&nbsp;
moments and we also do it as a way to model&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.600 --> 00:10:37.680
what some of those moments look&nbsp;
like and how you might describe them

00:10:40.800 --> 00:10:50.960
I think this book in some ways is my response&nbsp;
to seeing ordinary people all those years ago&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.880 --> 00:10:58.160
experiencing a film that reached out and&nbsp;
made connection with me as a human being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.080 --> 00:11:05.440
and this book is me reaching&nbsp;
back out into the world to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.320 --> 00:11:13.840
connect in the way that I was connected&nbsp;
with to help readers feel less alone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.120 --> 00:11:19.920
but this is a very specific group of readers&nbsp;
these are these are our filmmakers these are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.920 --> 00:11:27.840
our actors and writers and directors and novelists&nbsp;
these are the people who tell stories in our time

00:11:30.320 --> 00:11:34.400
so I consider it a massive&nbsp;
privilege to get to have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.400 --> 00:11:43.120
a voice helping to equip storytellers to&nbsp;
tell stories that that will help connect us&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.920 --> 00:11:52.640
was there any story you the two of you&nbsp;
struggled to maybe leave in or leave out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.640 --> 00:11:57.680
was there one that you felt whether I mean you&nbsp;
shared some incredibly personal stuff just here&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.680 --> 00:12:03.120
and I think it could be beneficial for so many&nbsp;
people but there's a risk right and and that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.680 --> 00:12:12.160
yeah yeah I think that Kathy struggled with how&nbsp;
much to tell about her relationship with her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.160 --> 00:12:21.680
dad which was not warm and close he he was a dad&nbsp;
who never ever said the words to her I love you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:23.120 --> 00:12:29.760
and she wants to honor her dad her dad was a good&nbsp;
man but he was not a warm man and so she struggled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.760 --> 00:12:36.880
with whether to put that story in her out of&nbsp;
the book and ultimately the stories in the book&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.000 --> 00:12:46.480
she did it in a way that was honoring to him&nbsp;
she actually drew on what she words she said&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.480 --> 00:12:59.840
at his funeral and for me I think speaking&nbsp;
of this moment that I can't remember a moment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:59.840 --> 00:13:09.040
that seems to be of abuse in my early&nbsp;
early childhood is that was one that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.960 --> 00:13:21.280
I hesitated to include and wanted to be very&nbsp;
kind of wise about how how I told that story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.280 --> 00:13:31.360
but it's also one of the stories i'm most&nbsp;
interested in sharing because I think it could be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:33.360 --> 00:13:43.840
somehow encouraging or beneficial to someone else&nbsp;
and I think I found my way to understanding that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:43.840 --> 00:13:50.240
story through films and documentaries that&nbsp;
I identified with and I was I was feeling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:52.000 --> 00:13:58.080
really powerful emotions watching documentaries&nbsp;
in ways that didn't make sense to me that I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:58.080 --> 00:14:07.280
should be reacting that way and watching&nbsp;
those films ended up leading me into a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:08.960 --> 00:14:15.840
counseling dialogue that has like really&nbsp;
helped me put my finger on the lies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:16.480 --> 00:14:25.440
that had had crippled me and allowed me&nbsp;
then to figure out what was actually true&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:28.000 --> 00:14:38.000
so that's my hope is that in in sharing these&nbsp;
stories stories become gifts that we give to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:39.040 --> 00:14:47.280
a reader to an audience and stories work in a&nbsp;
way that is almost magical I can't explain how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:47.280 --> 00:14:52.560
stories work how each of us can hear the same&nbsp;
story read the same book watch the same movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:53.200 --> 00:15:00.240
and we connect with it in our in our own way we're&nbsp;
moved by it in our own way we make very personal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:00.240 --> 00:15:08.000
application so as we send these stories out into&nbsp;
the world I don't know what what will happen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:09.120 --> 00:15:17.120
but I’m hopeful and I look forward&nbsp;
to getting some emails in return.

